\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Neutral Gauss's law as an averaging constraint}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{For the \vocabk{residual action}{fdip-0089}, the neutral
\newvocab{Gauss constraint} requires
##{R_gf=f\qquad\text{for every }g\in K.}
Its solution space is the [physical Hilbert space](fdip-0083)
#{\mathcal H_{\mathrm{phys}}=\mathcal H_{\mathrm{link}}^K}.
The orthogonal projector onto it is
##{P=\int_KR_g\,\extd g,}
with normalized Haar measure. For a finite group #{G}, this is
##{P=\frac1{|G|^{|V_s|}}\sum_{g\in G^{V_s}}R_g.}
For the [positive gauge transfer operator](fdip-0080),
#{TP=PT}; hence time evolution preserves the constraint.}

\proof{
\p{For finite #{G}, left translation permutes the sum, so
#{R_hPf=Pf} for every #{h}. If #{f} is invariant, every summand
is #{f}, so #{Pf=f}. These facts prove #{P^2=P} and identify
its image. Unitarity gives #{R_g^{\dagger}=R_{g^{-1}}}; inversion
permutes the sum, proving #{P^{\dagger}=P}. Thus #{P} is the
orthogonal projector.}

\p{For compact #{G}, the same identities use normalized Haar
invariance, inversion and Fubini. The integral on #{L^2}, its
boundedness and its image were proved in \ref{fdip-0083};
that theorem applies here without a finite-group assumption.
The transfer operator commutes with every #{R_g} by
\ref{fdip-0080}, so it commutes with the average.}
}

\p{The finite algebraic projection statement is
\leanref{Representation.isProj_averageMap}. It assumes an
invertible group order in the coefficient ring, as holds over
#{\mathbb C}. Unitarity gives the orthogonality used here;
the compact Haar and transfer assertions have the separate proofs
just cited. [Maschke averaging](fgap-001K) uses a related finite
average to produce invariant complements.}

\p{For a finite configuration set #{X}, an invariant function is
exactly a function constant on each gauge orbit. Consequently
##{\dim_{\mathbb C}\mathcal H_{\mathrm{phys}}=|X/K|.}
Indeed, the orbit indicators have disjoint support and form a
basis. For Haar probability on a finite set, dividing each indicator
by the square root of its orbit's probability makes this basis
orthonormal.}
